## Root Cause
The rapid-exit detector at \`dispatch-daemon.ts:2037-2104\` fires when:
- \`sessionDuration < RAPID_EXIT_THRESHOLD_MS\` (10 seconds)
- AND \`!hasAssistantEvent\`

Rate limit messages like "You've hit your limit · resets 11pm" ARE assistant events, so \`hasAssistantEvent = true\` and the detector explicitly does NOT fire. This means the most common rate limit format (assistant message with rate limit text) is never caught by the rapid-exit detector.

## What to Fix

Enhance the rapid-exit detector to also detect rate limits from assistant messages:

1. Track the content of the last assistant message (not just whether one exists)
2. On exit, if the session exited quickly (< threshold) and the last assistant message matches \`isRateLimitMessage()\`, treat it as a rate limit:
   - Roll back resumeCount
   - Apply fallback rate limit to all executables in the chain
   - Parse the reset time from the message content

```typescript
let lastAssistantContent: string | undefined;
const onEvent = (event: { type: string; message?: string }) => {
  if (event.type === 'assistant') {
    hasAssistantEvent = true;
    if (event.message) lastAssistantContent = event.message;
  }
};

// In onExit:
if (sessionDuration < RAPID_EXIT_THRESHOLD_MS) {
  if (!hasAssistantEvent) {
    // Existing: silent rate limit (HTTP 429)
  } else if (lastAssistantContent && isRateLimitMessage(lastAssistantContent)) {
    // NEW: Rate limit shown as assistant message
    // Same recovery: roll back resumeCount, apply rate limit
  }
}
```

This provides a safety net even if the primary rate limit detection path (Bug 2 fix) misses the event.

