## Problem

When `sf` is installed globally via pnpm, smithy CLI commands (`daemon`, `agent`, `dispatch`, `merge`, `pool`, `task`) are unavailable — "Unknown command" errors. This is because pnpm's strict module isolation prevents quarry's plugin loader from resolving `@stoneforge/smithy` via dynamic `import()`.

The server loader already has a workaround for this exact problem: `packages/smithy/src/bin/sf.ts` pre-registers `globalThis.__stoneforge_smithy` with `loadServer` and `webRoot` so quarry's `serve` command can find smithy without `import('@stoneforge/smithy')`. But the CLI plugin system was never given the same treatment.

## Root Cause

`packages/quarry/src/cli/plugin-loader.ts:109` does `await import(packageName)` to discover plugins. Under pnpm strict isolation, quarry can't resolve smithy — the import fails silently (`notFound: true`), and all smithy commands are skipped.

## Fix

1. **In `packages/smithy/src/bin/sf.ts`**: Extend the existing `globalThis.__stoneforge_smithy` to also include the CLI plugin:
   ```typescript
   import { cliPlugin } from '../cli/plugin.js';

   (globalThis as Record<string, unknown>).__stoneforge_smithy = {
     loadServer: () => import('../server/index.js'),
     webRoot: resolve(__dirname, '../../web'),
     cliPlugin,  // NEW: pre-register CLI plugin
   };
   ```

2. **In `packages/quarry/src/cli/plugin-loader.ts`**: Check for pre-registered plugins before attempting dynamic import. When loading `@stoneforge/smithy`, check `globalThis.__stoneforge_smithy?.cliPlugin` first. If found, use it and skip the dynamic import:
   ```typescript
   async function loadPlugin(packageName: string, verbose: boolean): Promise<PluginLoadResult> {
     // Check for pre-registered plugin (handles pnpm strict isolation)
     if (packageName === '@stoneforge/smithy') {
       const preRegistered = (globalThis as any).__stoneforge_smithy?.cliPlugin;
       if (preRegistered && isValidCLIPlugin(preRegistered)) {
         if (verbose) console.error(`[plugin] Loaded ${preRegistered.name}@${preRegistered.version} (pre-registered)`);
         return { packageName, success: true, plugin: preRegistered };
       }
     }
     // ... existing dynamic import logic
   }
   ```

   Alternatively, make this generic rather than smithy-specific: check a `globalThis.__stoneforge_plugins` map keyed by package name. Use your judgment on which approach is cleaner — the smithy-specific check is simpler and matches the existing `__stoneforge_smithy` pattern.

3. **Update `packages/smithy/dist/bin/sf.js`**: Rebuild so the dist output includes the `cliPlugin` pre-registration. (This happens automatically on build.)

