## Problem

The dispatch daemon enters an infinite rate limit sleep loop. After a 1-hour rate limit timer expires naturally, `hasRecentRateLimitPattern()` re-detects the same stale session history pattern and records a fresh 1-hour rate limit. This repeats forever.

The user reports: "the rate limit detector got triggered to sleep the daemon — we haven't hit a rate limit for hours."

## Root Cause

Commit c958f7d2 replaced the recency-relative-to-now check (added in cedf0a36) with a `lastWakeAt`-only check:

```typescript
// cedf0a36 added this (CORRECT — was removed by c958f7d2):
const mostRecentStart = new Date(recentSessions[recentSessions.length - 1].startedAt).getTime();
if (Date.now() - mostRecentStart > RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_GAP_MS) {
  return false; // Pattern is stale
}

// c958f7d2 replaced it with this (INCOMPLETE):
if (this.lastWakeAt) {
  // Only invalidates after MANUAL wake()
}
```

The `lastWakeAt` check only runs when the user manually calls `wake()`. When the rate limit timer expires naturally, `lastWakeAt` is never set. So `hasRecentRateLimitPattern()` re-matches the same old sessions from hours ago, recording a fresh rate limit every time the timer expires.

## Fix

**Both checks are needed.** Restore the recency check that was removed by c958f7d2, keeping the `lastWakeAt` check as well. In `hasRecentRateLimitPattern()` (dispatch-daemon.ts, ~line 2401), add the recency check BEFORE the `lastWakeAt` check:

```typescript
// Check that the pattern is recent — if the last rapid-exit session
// was long ago, the rate limit has likely expired and recovery should proceed.
const mostRecentStart = new Date(recentSessions[recentSessions.length - 1].startedAt).getTime();
if (Date.now() - mostRecentStart > RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_GAP_MS) {
  return false;
}

// If wake() was called, ignore patterns that predate the wake.
if (this.lastWakeAt) {
  if (mostRecentStart < this.lastWakeAt) {
    return false;
  }
}
```

Note: the `mostRecentStart` variable is computed once and shared between both checks for efficiency.

