Plausible custom events are not firing because of a race condition. The analytics.ts script checks if window.plausible is a function on DOMContentLoaded, but the Plausible script loads with defer and may not be ready yet. All events silently no-op.

## Bug

In apps/website/src/scripts/analytics.ts line 48:
  if (typeof window.plausible === 'function') { ... }

This check fails if Plausible hasn't loaded yet. The trackEvent function silently does nothing.

Same issue exists in apps/docs/ analytics setup.

## Fix

Replace the guard clause with Plausible's recommended queue pattern. At the TOP of analytics.ts (before any event setup), add:

  window.plausible = window.plausible || function(...args: any[]) {
    (window.plausible.q = window.plausible.q || []).push(args);
  };

This creates a queue that buffers events. When the real Plausible script loads, it processes the queue. No events are lost regardless of load order.

Then update the trackEvent function to just call window.plausible directly without the typeof check:

  function trackEvent(name: string, props?: Record<string, string>): void {
    window.plausible(name, props ? { props } : undefined);
  }

Apply the same fix to the docs site analytics in apps/docs/src/components/overrides/Head.astro (or wherever the docs analytics script lives).

## Files
- apps/website/src/scripts/analytics.ts — main fix
- apps/docs/src/components/overrides/Head.astro — same fix for docs site

## Verification
IMPORTANT: Do not mark complete until verified working.
- pnpm build succeeds for both apps/website/ and apps/docs/
- In the built HTML output, confirm the queue pattern appears BEFORE the Plausible script tag
- The trackEvent function no longer has a typeof guard

Step-by-step guide for adding a new route/page to either frontend app (`quarry-web` or `smithy-web`). Both apps use TanStack Router with React Query and follow the same conventions.

## Prerequisites

- Familiarity with [Quarry Web Reference](el-4iiz) and [Platform Reference](el-41ua)
- Understanding of React, TanStack Router, and React Query
- Dev server running: `bun run --filter @stoneforge/{app} dev`

## Step 1: Create the Route Directory

Both apps follow the same convention. Create a directory under `apps/{app}/src/routes/{page-name}/`:

```
routes/{page-name}/
├── index.tsx        # Page component (required)
├── types.ts         # Route-specific types
├── hooks.ts         # Route-specific hooks
├── constants.ts     # Constants
├── utils.ts         # Utility functions
└── components/      # Route-specific components
    ├── index.ts     # Re-exports
    └── *.tsx        # Individual components
```

Only `index.tsx` is required — add the other files as complexity grows.

### Page Component Template

```tsx
// apps/{app}/src/routes/{page-name}/index.tsx
import { useSearch } from '@tanstack/react-router';
import { PageHeader } from '../../components/shared/PageHeader';
import { MyFeatureList } from './components';

export function MyFeaturePage() {
  const search = useSearch({ strict: false });

  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col h-full">
      <PageHeader title="My Feature" />
      <div className="flex-1 overflow-auto p-4">
        <MyFeatureList selected={search.selected} />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Route-Specific Types

```typescript
// apps/{app}/src/routes/{page-name}/types.ts
export interface MyFeatureSearchParams {
  selected?: string;
  page?: number;
  limit?: number;
}
```

### Route-Specific Hooks

```typescript
// apps/{app}/src/routes/{page-name}/hooks.ts
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

export function useMyFeatureData(id?: string) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['my-feature', id],
    queryFn: async () => {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/my-feature/${id}`);
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch');
      return res.json();
    },
    enabled: !!id,
  });
}
```

## Step 2: Register the Route

Add the route to `apps/{app}/src/router.tsx`.

### 2a. Import the Page Component

```typescript
// At the top of router.tsx, with other imports
import { MyFeaturePage } from './routes/my-feature';
```

### 2b. Create the Route with Search Param Validation

```typescript
const myFeatureRoute = createRoute({
  getParentRoute: () => rootRoute,
  path: '/my-feature',
  component: MyFeaturePage,
  validateSearch: (search: Record<string, unknown>) => {
    return {
      selected: typeof search.selected === 'string' ? search.selected : undefined,
      page: typeof search.page === 'number' ? search.page :
            typeof search.page === 'string' ? parseInt(search.page, 10) || 1 : 1,
      limit: typeof search.limit === 'number' ? search.limit :
             typeof search.limit === 'string' ? parseInt(search.limit, 10) || 25 : 25,
    };
  },
});
```

**Key points:**
- `getParentRoute: () => rootRoute` makes the page render inside the `AppShell` layout
- `validateSearch` ensures type-safe URL search params with sensible defaults
- Always handle both `number` and `string` types for numeric params (browser URL params arrive as strings)

### 2c. Add to the Route Tree

Find the `routeTree` definition and add your route:

```typescript
const routeTree = rootRoute.addChildren([
  // ... existing routes
  myFeatureRoute,
]);
```

### Optional: Redirect Route

If you need to redirect from another path:

```typescript
const legacyRoute = createRoute({
  getParentRoute: () => rootRoute,
  path: '/old-path',
  beforeLoad: () => {
    throw redirect({ to: '/my-feature' });
  },
});
```

## Step 3: Add Sidebar Navigation

Add a navigation link so users can find your page.

### Sidebar File Locations

| App | Sidebar File |
|-----|-------------|
| smithy-web | `apps/smithy-web/src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx` |
| quarry-web | `apps/quarry-web/src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx` |

### 3a. Import the Icon

```typescript
// Add to existing lucide-react imports
import {
  // ... existing icons
  Sparkles,  // Your icon choice
} from 'lucide-react';
```

Browse available icons at [lucide.dev](https://lucide.dev).

### 3b. Add the Nav Item

Find the `NAV_SECTIONS` array and add your item to the appropriate section:

**smithy-web pattern:**
```typescript
const NAV_SECTIONS = [
  {
    id: 'work',
    label: 'Work',
    defaultExpanded: true,
    items: [
      // ... existing items
      {
        to: '/my-feature',
        icon: Sparkles,
        label: 'My Feature',
        testId: 'nav-my-feature',
        actionId: 'nav.myFeature',  // Optional: keyboard shortcut ID
      },
    ],
  },
  // ... other sections
];
```

**quarry-web pattern** (supports badges):
```typescript
{
  to: '/my-feature',
  icon: Sparkles,
  label: 'My Feature',
  testId: 'nav-my-feature',
  actionId: 'nav.myFeature',
  // Optional: show a badge count (currently only 'inbox' is supported)
  // badgeKey: 'inbox',
},
```

### Sidebar Sections

| smithy-web Sections | quarry-web Sections |
|---------------------|---------------------|
| Overview | Dashboard |
| Work | Work |
| Orchestration | Collaborate |
| Collaborate | Organize |
| Analytics | — |

Bottom nav items (e.g., Settings) are defined separately in `BOTTOM_NAV_ITEMS`.

## Step 4: Wire API Hooks

If your page fetches data from the API, create hooks in the centralized hooks directory.

### Hook File Locations

| App | Hooks Directory |
|-----|----------------|
| smithy-web | `apps/smithy-web/src/api/hooks/` |
| quarry-web | `apps/quarry-web/src/api/hooks/` |

### 4a. Create the Hook File

```typescript
// apps/{app}/src/api/hooks/useMyFeature.ts
import { useQuery, useMutation, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';

const API_BASE = import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL || '';

// Query key factory for cache management
export const myFeatureKeys = {
  all: ['my-feature'] as const,
  list: (filter?: MyFeatureFilter) => [...myFeatureKeys.all, 'list', filter] as const,
  detail: (id: string) => [...myFeatureKeys.all, 'detail', id] as const,
};

// Fetch function
async function fetchMyFeatures(filter?: MyFeatureFilter) {
  const params = new URLSearchParams();
  if (filter?.status) params.set('status', filter.status);
  if (filter?.limit) params.set('limit', String(filter.limit));

  const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/my-feature?${params}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch');
  return res.json();
}

// Query hook
export function useMyFeatures(filter?: MyFeatureFilter) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: myFeatureKeys.list(filter),
    queryFn: () => fetchMyFeatures(filter),
    staleTime: 30_000,
    refetchOnWindowFocus: true,
  });
}

// Mutation hook
export function useCreateMyFeature() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();

  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: async (data: CreateMyFeatureInput) => {
      const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE}/api/my-feature`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify(data),
      });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to create');
      return res.json();
    },
    onSuccess: () => {
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: myFeatureKeys.all });
    },
  });
}
```

### 4b. Export from Index

```typescript
// apps/{app}/src/api/hooks/index.ts
export * from './useMyFeature';
```

> **Note:** `smithy-web` uses barrel re-exports (`api/hooks/index.ts`). `quarry-web` does not have a barrel file — import hooks directly by path (e.g., `import { useMyFeatures } from '../api/hooks/useMyFeature'`).

### 4c. Add Types (if needed)

```typescript
// apps/{app}/src/api/types.ts
export interface MyFeatureFilter {
  status?: string;
  limit?: number;
}

export interface CreateMyFeatureInput {
  title: string;
  description?: string;
}
```

> **Note:** `smithy-web` has a centralized `api/types.ts`. `quarry-web` co-locates types with route-specific files (e.g., `routes/{page-name}/types.ts`) — add your types there instead.

### 4d. Add Real-Time Updates (Optional)

Use the `useRealtimeEvents` hook to invalidate queries when data changes via WebSocket:

```typescript
import { useRealtimeEvents } from '../../api/hooks/useRealtimeEvents';

function MyFeaturePage() {
  useRealtimeEvents({
    channels: ['tasks'], // Channel names are strings: 'tasks', 'documents', 'entities', '*' (default), etc.
    onEvent: (event) => {
      // React Query caches are automatically invalidated
      // via the defaultQueryKeyMapper in useRealtimeEvents
    },
  });

  // ... rest of component
}
```

## Step 5: Add Search Param Validation

Search params let users bookmark and share filtered views. The `validateSearch` function in the route definition ensures type safety.

### Common Patterns

```typescript
validateSearch: (search: Record<string, unknown>) => {
  return {
    // String param (optional)
    selected: typeof search.selected === 'string' ? search.selected : undefined,

    // String param with default
    tab: typeof search.tab === 'string' ? search.tab : 'overview',

    // Number param with default
    page: typeof search.page === 'number' ? search.page :
          typeof search.page === 'string' ? parseInt(search.page, 10) || 1 : 1,

    // Boolean param
    readyOnly: search.readyOnly === true || search.readyOnly === 'true',

    // Enum param
    status: ['active', 'completed', 'archived'].includes(search.status as string)
      ? (search.status as string) : undefined,
  };
},
```

### Using Search Params in Components

```tsx
import { useSearch, useNavigate } from '@tanstack/react-router';

function MyFeaturePage() {
  const { selected, page, limit } = useSearch({ strict: false });
  const navigate = useNavigate();

  const handleSelect = (id: string) => {
    navigate({ search: (prev) => ({ ...prev, selected: id }) });
  };

  const handlePageChange = (newPage: number) => {
    navigate({ search: (prev) => ({ ...prev, page: newPage }) });
  };
}
```

