## Bug

When the merge steward merges a task branch, if `git push origin HEAD:<targetBranch>` fails, the error is silently swallowed and `mergeBranch()` returns `{ success: true }`. This causes the steward to mark the task as merged and clean up the branch, but the commits never reached origin. The merge commit existed only in a temp worktree that gets cleaned up immediately after.

## Root Cause

`packages/smithy/src/git/merge.ts`, lines 429-437:
```typescript
if (autoPush && !localOnly) {
  try {
    await execGitSafe(`push origin HEAD:${targetBranch}`, mergeDir, workspaceRoot);
  } catch (pushError) {
    const pushErrorMsg = pushError instanceof Error ? pushError.message : String(pushError);
    console.warn(`[git/merge] Failed to push to remote: ${pushErrorMsg}`);
    // BUG: push failure is logged but not reflected in the result
  }
}
mergeResult = { success: true, commitHash, hasConflict: false }; // Still reports success!
```

## Fix

### 1. Return failure when push fails

When push fails and `autoPush` is true (meaning the caller expects the push to happen), return a failure result instead of success:

```typescript
if (autoPush && !localOnly) {
  try {
    await execGitSafe(`push origin HEAD:${targetBranch}`, mergeDir, workspaceRoot);
  } catch (pushError) {
    const pushErrorMsg = pushError instanceof Error ? pushError.message : String(pushError);
    mergeResult = {
      success: false,
      commitHash,
      hasConflict: false,
      error: `Merge commit created but push to origin failed: ${pushErrorMsg}`,
    };
    // Skip setting the success result below — jump to finally for cleanup
    // Use a flag or restructure the flow
  }
}
```

One clean approach: use a `pushFailed` variable:
```typescript
let pushFailed = false;
if (autoPush && !localOnly) {
  try {
    await execGitSafe(`push origin HEAD:${targetBranch}`, mergeDir, workspaceRoot);
  } catch (pushError) {
    const pushErrorMsg = pushError instanceof Error ? pushError.message : String(pushError);
    pushFailed = true;
    mergeResult = {
      success: false,
      commitHash,
      hasConflict: false,
      error: `Merge succeeded locally but push to origin/ failed: ${pushErrorMsg}. The merge commit (${commitHash}) was not delivered to the remote. Retry the push or re-merge.`,
    };
  }
}
if (!pushFailed) {
  mergeResult = { success: true, commitHash, hasConflict: false };
}
```

### 2. Add post-push verification

After a successful push, verify that the commit actually landed on the remote:
```typescript
if (autoPush && !localOnly) {
  try {
    await execGitSafe(`push origin HEAD:${targetBranch}`, mergeDir, workspaceRoot);

    // Verify push landed
    await execAsync(`git fetch origin ${targetBranch}`, { cwd: workspaceRoot, encoding: 'utf8' });
    const { stdout: remoteHash } = await execAsync(
      `git rev-parse origin/${targetBranch}`,
      { cwd: workspaceRoot, encoding: 'utf8' }
    );
    const { stdout: isAncestor } = await execAsync(
      `git merge-base --is-ancestor ${commitHash} origin/${targetBranch}`,
      { cwd: workspaceRoot, encoding: 'utf8' }
    ).catch(() => ({ stdout: '' }));
    // If the command exits 0, commitHash is an ancestor of origin/targetBranch — push confirmed
  } catch (pushError) {
    // ... return failure as above
  }
}
```

Note: `git merge-base --is-ancestor` exits 0 if true, non-zero if false. So wrap in try/catch and treat non-zero as verification failure.

