## Context

The arrow-key workflow preset selector in \`sf init\` (implemented in task el-tvig1t) has a rendering bug: pressing arrow keys duplicates menu options instead of redrawing in place.

**IMPORTANT**: This fix must be applied on top of the worker branch:
\`agent/joe-claude/el-tvig1t-sf-init-replace-text-input-wit\`

## Bug

When the user presses up/down arrow keys, the menu options get duplicated. The screenshot shows "Auto" appearing 5 times, with the full 3-option menu appended below. Each keypress appends more lines instead of overwriting.

## Root Cause

**File:** \`packages/quarry/src/cli/commands/init.ts\`, function \`renderPresetMenu()\` (around line 415)

The redraw logic uses \`\\x1B[F\\x1B[2K\` (cursor previous line + clear line) in a loop of \`presets.length\` (3) iterations to move up and clear the previous render before drawing again:

\`\`\`typescript
if (!isInitialRender) {
  for (let i = 0; i < presets.length; i++) {
    stdout.write('\\x1B[F\\x1B[2K');
  }
}
\`\`\`

This assumes each preset occupies exactly 1 terminal line. But the description lines are long:
\`\`\`
  ❯ Auto      — Agents merge directly to main. Fast iteration, no human review.
\`\`\`

In narrow terminals, these lines **wrap to 2+ physical lines**. The code moves up 3 logical lines, but the actual content spans 5-6 physical lines due to wrapping. The cursor doesn't go back far enough, so old content remains and new content is appended below it.

## Fix

Replace the line-by-line cursor movement with a cursor-save/restore + clear-to-end approach that handles wrapping correctly:

**Option A (recommended):** Track the cursor start position and use clear-to-end-of-screen:

\`\`\`typescript
function renderPresetMenu(
  presets: WorkflowPreset[],
  selectedIndex: number,
  isInitialRender: boolean
): void {
  const { stdout } = process;

  if (!isInitialRender) {
    // Move cursor up to the saved start row and clear everything below
    // Use columns to calculate actual physical lines consumed
    const cols = stdout.columns || 80;
    let physicalLines = 0;
    for (const p of presets) {
      const label = p.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + p.slice(1);
      const line = \`    \${label.padEnd(10)}— \${PRESET_DESCRIPTIONS[p]}\`;
      physicalLines += Math.ceil(line.length / cols);
    }
    // Move up the correct number of physical lines
    stdout.write(\`\\x1B[\${physicalLines}A\`);
    // Clear from cursor to end of screen (handles any leftover wrapped content)
    stdout.write('\\x1B[J');
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < presets.length; i++) {
    const p = presets[i];
    const label = p.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + p.slice(1);
    const prefix = i === selectedIndex ? '  ❯ ' : '    ';
    stdout.write(\`\${prefix}\${label.padEnd(10)}— \${PRESET_DESCRIPTIONS[p]}\\n\`);
  }
}
\`\`\`

Key changes:
1. Calculate the number of **physical** terminal lines each option occupies using \`stdout.columns\`
2. Move cursor up by the total physical line count: \`\\x1B[\${physicalLines}A\`
3. Clear from cursor to end of screen: \`\\x1B[J\` (this handles any extra wrapped content below)

**Option B (simpler but less portable):** Use \`\\x1B7\` (save cursor) before initial render, \`\\x1B8\` (restore cursor) + \`\\x1B[J\` (clear to end) before re-render. Note: \`\\x1B7\`/\`\\x1B8\` (DEC save/restore) are more portable than \`\\x1B[s\`/\`\\x1B[u\` (SCO save/restore).

